  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In a photoelectric effect experiment, the graph of stopping potential <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math> versus reciprocal of wavelength obtained is shown in the figure. As the intensity of incident radiation is increased :&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/58673523-de47-4939-936d-71af542b197f-image.png\" style=\"width: 246px; height: 230px;\" /></p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "y9WL8",
      "text": "Straight line shifts to right",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "YmEvb",
      "text": "Slope of the straight line get more steep",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "RmjiN",
      "text": "Straight line shifts to left",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "UtDwW",
      "text": "Graph does not change",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Einstein&#39;s photoelectric equation is given by,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>eV</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>&#957;</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;is the work function.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>e&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mfrac></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#957;</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></math>.</p><p>Intensity does not affect the frequency and work function of a material. Therefore,&nbsp;the graph does not change with increasing intensity.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
